WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.720
Array bounds checking matters in C because the language gives developers direct control over memory while relying on code to stay inside valid object boundaries.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:16.180
That control is one reason C remains useful for systems programming, embedded work, performance-sensitive components, and low-level libraries.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:23.840
It also means an array access that looks ordinary can become unsafe if the index, length, offset, or capacity was misunderstood.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.900
C does not automatically check every array access at runtime.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:33.480
An expression such as an indexed read or write depends on the programmer's reasoning about the valid range.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:39.140
If that reasoning is wrong, the program may read from or write to memory outside the intended array.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.920
The result may vary by build, platform, data shape, and surrounding code.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:53.900
Out-of-bounds reads can produce unreliable results, expose unintended data to later logic, or make decisions based on bytes that are not part of the intended object.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:59.400
Even when the program does not crash, it may be using data that the developer never meant to consider.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:04.820
That is enough to break validation, parsing, comparison, logging, or control decisions.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:14.080
Out-of-bounds writes are often more damaging because they can corrupt nearby state, change a length field, disturb a pointer, break a data structure, or crash the process.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:18.940
The safe way to discuss this in engineering terms is not to dwell on misuse.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:25.480
It is to recognize that a single unchecked write can make the program's future behavior untrustworthy.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:39.140
Most bounds problems begin as ordinary engineering mistakes: a wrong loop condition, a count confused with capacity, an unchecked input length, an offset added before validation, or an assumption that a terminator exists.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:45.280
Defensive C treats indexes, lengths, offsets, and capacities as first-class design concerns.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.580
The rest of this course builds habits for making those concerns visible and reviewable.
